language_detection
  rule rule_russian
    language ru
    source-host "://ru.wikipedia.org/"
    label Cyrillic
    script Cyrillic
    start 0x0400
    end 0x04FF
    markers ("что" "как" "кто" "где" "когда" "почему")
    note "Catches Russian, Belarusian, Ukrainian, Bulgarian — all Cyrillic-block scripts route to the ru response set."
  rule rule_hindi
    language hi
    source-host "://hi.wikipedia.org/"
    label Devanagari
    script Devanagari
    start 0x0900
    end 0x097F
    markers ("क्या" "कौन" "कहाँ" "कब" "कैसे" "क्यों")
    note "Catches Hindi, Marathi, Sanskrit — Devanagari is the shared script."
  rule rule_chinese
    language zh
    source-host "://zh.wikipedia.org/"
    label "CJK Unified Ideographs"
    script Han
    start 0x4E00
    end 0x9FFF
    markers ("什么" "吗" "怎么" "谁" "哪")
    note "Catches Simplified and Traditional Chinese. Worker normalises to the zh response set."
  rule rule_spanish
    language es
    source-host "://es.wikipedia.org/"
    label "Latin-1 Supplement"
    script Latin
    start 0x00C0
    end 0x00FF
    markers ("¿" "¡" "ñ" "qué" "cómo" "quién" "dónde" "cuándo" "cuál" "hola" "gracias" "por favor")
    note "Spanish shares the Latin script with the fallback language, so its range cannot decide a prompt on its own — the markers below carry the vote and the range only widens the Latin count to the accented letters. Registered as seed data only (issue #706): no Rust edit was needed to add the fifth language."
  rule rule_english
    language en
    source-host "://en.wikipedia.org/"
    label "Basic Latin"
    script Latin
    fallback yes
    alphabetic-only yes
    start 0x0041
    end 0x007A
    note "Fallback / default. Worker also returns en when no Unicode block above matches. `fallback yes` makes the default language a seed record instead of a Rust constant; `alphabetic-only yes` keeps the six ASCII punctuation code points inside 0x0041-0x007A out of the Latin count."
